an operator owns the specification phase\" and \"The human operator guides spec development through make-work and do-work skills.\" This means PRD creation is explicitly a human task. But the planning_constitution does include documentation deliverables with PRD format. So it seems like PRDs CAN be tasks.\n\nBut wait — the project's whole purpose is to test AI code generation from human-written specs. The specs are supposed to be human-authored. So I shouldn't propose spec-writing tasks.\n\nLet me check the design decisions again: the operator doesn't write Go, and the orchestrator doesn't write PRDs. The stitch agent is part of the code generation pipeline, so it should only generate Go code in the cmd/ and pkg/ directories. That means the only remaining work with ready specs is rel04.\n\nNow I'm checking what's actually implemented in the ls command — it has basic flags like -a, -A, -1, -C, and --color, but it's missing the extended features like -l, -r, -t, -S, -R, -d, and -h. So the ls-extended use case is a valid target since those features aren't in the codebase yet.\n\nThe status documents mention that specs for both core and extended ls are complete, but I don't have access to the actual PRD for ls-extended in the project context, which makes it harder to anchor specific requirements for the tasks I'm creating.\n\nLooking at the orphaned PRDs and existing code, there's some inconsistency—true and false have PRDs that exist but aren't reflected in the roadmap, yet their implementations are already done. For the other commands like yes, basename, dirname, tee, head, and seq, I can see they're pending specs, but I need to figure out what PRD references are actually available to work with. I'm wondering whether ls-extended should be under the same PRD as the core ls implementation or if it needs its own. The specs are marked as written and the code status shows spec_complete for both releases, but I don't have the actual PRD content to work with. I'm realizing t